# Literature Review
## LLM-SR: SCIENTIFIC EQUATION DISCOVERY VIA
PROGRAMMING WITH LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS
https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.18400
Talks about using LLMs to generate candidates for numerical optimisation, but rely on scientific priors within token embeddings

## DrSR: LLM based Scientific Equation Discovery with
Dual Reasoning from Data and Experience
https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.04282

## LLM-based Symbolic Regression with Tool-Augmented Multi-Objective Optimization
https://openreview.net/pdf?id=0L4RWQV8Qa
NOT ACCEPTED YET, but similar to DrSR

# Work done
Finally adapted Denario to a free offline model. This required significant changes to cmbagent as well

## Full run for Denario with SR
I've tested a full run with Offline Denario with SR with the test prompt, which isn't really indicative since the experimenter already has the code to generate the data,
but still can be run as a sanity check.
Firstly, there was a problem with the experiment module saving files in the wrong place. This suggests that there may still be some errors varying between runs.
When the SR module finally ran, it took a while.
So for this run of the SR module, I tried to use the same method as the experimenter, where the engineer writes the code to load everything into PySR.
This is causing it to be quite slow, since there are multiple rounds of planning and debugging. This could be solved with a cap on the number of feedback rounds.
However another method would just be to have a module that takes the data generated and does SR "manually", then use an agent to determine if it makes sense or not.
I wanted to integrate an interative process like in DrSR, which would require an agentic caller, I think? 
